<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip Melanchthon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1497">1497</a>–<a href="/years/1560">1560</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Philipp Melanchthon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip Melanchthon was a German Lutheran reformer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s collaborator and the first systematic theologian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>. A scholar of Greek and a Christian humanist, he became professor of Greek at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> in 1518, and his Loci communes of 1521 was the first systematic account of its teaching. He was the main author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a>, presented at the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1530, and through his grammars, textbooks and work in education he became the founder of the learned schools of Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon.jpg" alt="Philip Melanchthon" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder and workshop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhilippMelanchthon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
